{n:"Periodic Trends",e:"📈",tip:["Draw the arrows: atomic radius goes UP down a group and DOWN across a period. Ionization energy and electronegativity do the opposite: DOWN a group, UP across.","Memory trick: Fluorine is the king. Top right corner (ignore noble gases) = highest IE and EN, smallest radius.","Always give reasons: more protons = stronger nuclear charge (across). More energy levels = more shielding and distance (down)."]},
{n:"Families and Groups",e:"🧪",tip:["Valence electrons = the A-group digit: alkali 1, alkaline earth 2, halogens 7, noble gases 8 (He has 2).","Make a flashcard map of the table with group numbers: alkali 1, alkaline earth 2, halogens 17, noble gases 18.","Octet = 8 valence electrons = stable. Atoms bond to reach it."]},
{n:"Ions and Atoms",e:"⚡",tip:["Cation < Atom < Anion. Losing electrons shrinks you; gaining electrons swells you.","Charge from the table: group 1 is +1, 2 is +2, 13 is +3, 15 is −3, 16 is −2, 17 is −1.","Protons never change when ions form, only electrons. Practice by drawing Bohr models."]},
{n:"Bonding",e:"🔗",tip:["Metal + metal = metallic (sea of electrons). Metal + nonmetal = ionic (transfer). Write this on a sticky note.","Crisscross: the charge number of one ion becomes the subscript of the other, then reduce. Practice 10 of these.","Diatomics: remember H, O, N, Cl, Br, I, F (\"Have No Fear Of Ice Cold Beer\")."]},
{n:"Naming Compounds",e:"🏷️",tip:["Roman numerals only for transition metals and PASS elements, because they can have more than one charge.","Name order: cation first, anion second (-ide ending). Polyatomic ions keep their own names.","Make flashcards for polyatomics (NO₃⁻, SO₄²⁻, OH⁻, NH₄⁺) and drill them daily."]}
];
